  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how a dielectric elastomer generator (DEG) works and how it can be represented electrically. In the top schematic, a water droplet contacts the dielectric surface near an electrode. Charges accumulate at the water–dielectric interface, forming opposite charge layers. As the droplet spreads, shifts, and then leaves, electrons flow through the external circuit, showing that mechanical interaction of the droplet with the dielectric causes charge redistribution and current generation. The sequence emphasizes a contact/separation process: the droplet changes the interfacial charge balance, and the connected electrodes respond by transferring charge.\n\nThe lower part connects this physical picture to an equivalent circuit. A close-up of the interface shows ions in the water and a dense interfacial charge layer above the dielectric. The device is then modeled using a dielectric capacitance together with two electrical double-layer capacitances, a water resistance, a gate-like voltage source, and an external load. The circuit labels source, drain, and gate, making the DEG look somewhat transistor-like, where the droplet and interfacial electrochemistry modulate the electrical response. Overall, the figure links droplet-induced interfacial charging to a practical lumped-element circuit model.",
